ake that as a  required parameter  and for now let's just select a system  entity number  although in real life pugs would  probably have a custom entity with a  certain sequence of numbers that would  be matched  to their backend through the web hook at  this state  we check if the order number is received  by adding the condition  page.params.status equals final  once the condition is met we want to  transition to  another page to provide a return  confirmation  at the return confirmation page all we  do is send a confirmation  saying your return is processed adding  our session parameter or a number  in there using the dollar sign and we  transition from here to the end page  although we could transition to another  flow as well if you wanted the user to  say  take a survey or order more things  there's a lot more that you can do in  returns you could add more parameters to  collect  connect to the pugs back-end api but i  will leave that to you to explore since  the point of this video is to get you  familiar with the cx console and the  concepts  now we know that pugs has multiple teams  working on the flows  they need the ability to save progress  by creating different versions  and then deploy specific versions of  their flows to development  testing or production environments let  me show you how versions and  environments work in pugs  agent we go to the manage tab and in  deployment we find  versions now here since pugs has  multiple flows  each flow can have its own version so  the teams can independently manage their  own versions  versions are immutable snapshots of the  flow data  and associated agent data like intents  entities  web hooks pages route groups and a lot  more  now if we click on a flow here let's say  return  create a version give it a name and  description  and give it a few seconds to become  ready and if we want to use this version  then we can just load it in our draft  slow by clicking on this button  but let's make another version 